1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,816
How does a buyer reach the bank's payment page, and nobody else?

2
00:00:03,866 --> 00:00:10,464
In Lesson 25, the create request made exactly one payment, and ecom answered with a link.

3
00:00:10,514 --> 00:00:14,544
In Lesson 5, the bank's page held the card frame from the card zone.

4
00:00:14,594 --> 00:00:18,624
This lesson builds that page, so only the right buyer can open it.

5
00:00:18,674 --> 00:00:20,616
The merchant has created a payment.

6
00:00:20,666 --> 00:00:22,488
Now the buyer has to pay it.

7
00:00:22,538 --> 00:00:27,648
The buyer must not type a card on the shop's own site, because the shop must never touch a card number.

8
00:00:27,698 --> 00:00:32,448
And the page cannot sit at an address anyone could guess by changing a number.

9
00:00:32,498 --> 00:00:35,208
It must open only for the person who got the link.

10
00:00:35,258 --> 00:00:37,080
It must work only for a while.

11
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:39,456
It must show exactly what is being paid.

12
00:00:39,506 --> 00:00:42,768
And it must survive a buyer who clicks Confirm twice.

13
00:00:42,818 --> 00:00:44,640
Think of a hotel key card.

14
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:46,512
It opens one room.

15
00:00:46,562 --> 00:00:48,432
It works for the guest who holds it.

16
00:00:48,482 --> 00:00:50,328
And it stops working at checkout.

17
00:00:50,378 --> 00:00:52,824
The bank's payment page works the same way.

18
00:00:52,874 --> 00:00:56,208
When the merchant creates a payment, the bank answers with a link.

19
00:00:56,258 --> 00:01:01,104
The link carries a page token: a long, random, one-time key.

20
00:01:01,154 --> 00:01:03,408
The merchant sends the buyer to that link.

21
00:01:03,458 --> 00:01:06,072
The bank does not keep the token itself.

22
00:01:06,122 --> 00:01:12,096
It keeps only a fingerprint of it, so even someone reading the bank's records cannot rebuild the link.

23
00:01:12,146 --> 00:01:15,264
And the link expires after the bank's chosen time.

24
00:01:15,314 --> 00:01:18,840
When the buyer opens the link, the bank's payment page loads.

25
00:01:18,890 --> 00:01:25,080
It shows the website's name and logo, the order, the amount, and the cards this website accepts.

26
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:27,768
Those are the cards with a live terminal behind them.

27
00:01:27,818 --> 00:01:34,056
Then the page opens a card-entry session with the card zone, and puts the card zone's frame inside the page.

28
00:01:34,106 --> 00:01:37,872
The card number goes into that frame, and only into that frame.

29
00:01:37,922 --> 00:01:41,664
A payment gets only the bank's chosen number of these sessions.

30
00:01:41,714 --> 00:01:45,336
After that, the payment fails, and no money moves.

31
00:01:45,386 --> 00:01:50,136
When the buyer finishes typing, the frame sends the page one small message.

32
00:01:50,186 --> 00:01:55,224
The page accepts it only from the frame's own address, and only if it holds a token.

33
00:01:55,274 --> 00:01:57,096
It never accepts a card number.

34
00:01:57,146 --> 00:01:59,376
The page passes the token to ecom.

35
00:01:59,426 --> 00:02:03,480
ecom asks the card zone whether this card belongs to this payment's session.

36
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:05,376
Only then does the charge begin.

37
00:02:05,426 --> 00:02:09,936
And a page lock lets only one step run at a time for each payment.

38
00:02:09,986 --> 00:02:12,120
Now watch the product from the buyer's side.

39
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:14,664
The buyer taps the link in the shop's checkout.

40
00:02:14,714 --> 00:02:18,864
The page opens with a line at the top: secure payment page of the bank.

41
00:02:18,914 --> 00:02:25,872
Below it are the website, the order number, the amount to pay, the accepted cards, and a line that says valid until, with the time.

42
00:02:25,922 --> 00:02:28,200
Under that sits the card details frame.

43
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,072
The buyer double-clicks Confirm.

44
00:02:30,122 --> 00:02:31,944
The first click goes through.

45
00:02:31,994 --> 00:02:35,520
The second is turned away, because the page is busy.

46
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:37,392
Only one step runs.

47
00:02:37,442 --> 00:02:42,144
Suppose the buyer leaves the frame open too long, or makes too many tries.

48
00:02:42,194 --> 00:02:45,864
The page says that attempt ended, and asks to enter the card again.

49
00:02:45,914 --> 00:02:53,784
If the buyer uses up every attempt, the page says the payment page closed before any money moved, and asks for a new link from the shop.

50
00:02:53,834 --> 00:02:57,672
And a buyer who opens an old link sees that the link has expired.

51
00:02:57,722 --> 00:03:01,608
When the payment ends, the page shows a back to the shop link.

52
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:03,480
What does this protect?

53
00:03:03,530 --> 00:03:08,592
Without the page token, anyone could walk through payment pages by guessing addresses.

54
00:03:08,642 --> 00:03:13,104
Without the fingerprint, a leaked copy of the bank's records would hand out live links.

55
00:03:13,154 --> 00:03:17,160
Without the frame, the card number would pass through ecom and the shop.

56
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:20,856
Without the lock, one double click could start two charges.

57
00:03:20,906 --> 00:03:24,720
The page protects the buyer, who sees exactly what is being paid.

58
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:27,624
It protects the shop, which never touches a card.

59
00:03:27,674 --> 00:03:29,496
So back to the key card.

60
00:03:29,546 --> 00:03:32,472
One guest, one room, until checkout.

61
00:03:32,522 --> 00:03:34,584
Next: the charge itself.
